WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:26.510
Oh, hi.

9
00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:32.250
Welcome, everyone, to a breakdown of Lotto.

10
00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:33.710
Yes.

11
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.010
Are you guys ready to get lucky?

12
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:39.930
It's Season 8, Episode 3.

13
00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:50.230
It was written by Charlie Grandy, directed by John Krasinski, and was an episode that received a solid B rating from reviewer Miles McNutt.

14
00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:51.450
Way to go.

15
00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.510
Would you like a summary?

16
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.110
You know I would.

17
00:00:55.570 --> 00:01:03.350
When the warehouse crew win the lottery and quit their jobs, the upstairs employees are left to pick up the slack until a new crew can be hired.

18
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:10.590
Aaron, Jim, Dwight, and Kevin can't seem to figure out how to load boxes of paper into a delivery truck.

19
00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.550
It takes them all day.

20
00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:14.610
And then they don't even finish.

21
00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.190
All day.

22
00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:19.470
And not just all day, but they become like Wile E.

23
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.070
Coyote trying to build some kind of device to load them.

24
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:25.650
It's just, it's bonkers.

25
00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.970
In the meantime, a very depressed Daryl issues an ultimatum to Andy.

26
00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.050
He says, fire me or make me manager.

27
00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:41.890
And then the rest of the gang fantasize about what they would do if they had win the lottery, which causes a little friction between Pam and Jim.

28
00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:43.590
They have dueling fantasies.

29
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.790
Yeah, they don't get along in their fantasy lives.

30
00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.290
They don't.

31
00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:55.510
Which normally you would think that your marriage is even better and stronger in your fantasy life, but not so.

32
00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:00.990
Or in your fantasy life, is it more clear because you have options?

33
00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:02.830
Oh.

34
00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:03.550
Oh.

35
00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:08.010
That's a very like therapist-y thing to say, Angela.

36
00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:10.550
I like that.

37
00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:11.770
I love that thought.

38
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.270
Journal on that, everybody.

39
00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:14.070
Mm-hmm.

40
00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:25.830
Well, listen, we don't have any fast facts today because instead we have an amazing interview with Mark Brooks, who plays Nate, who is featured in this episode,

41
00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:30.910
but who also appears in 19 episodes of The Office, spanning three seasons.

42
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.730
We can't be more excited for you to hear this interview.

43
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:35.850
He's an absolute delight of a person.

44
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:36.570
Yes.

45
00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:44.730
We have wanted to talk to him for a while, and this is not the last time we're talking to him because we have more to ask Mark about as the seasons progress.

46
00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:52.970
And I know that we always say that everybody is so nice and amazing, but I think you're going to hear in this interview that Mark is like at the top.

47
00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:53.430
Yeah.

48
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:54.270
He's the beSt.

49
00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.110
He's 100% real deal.

50
00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:57.530
And here you go.

51
00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.290
Here's our conversation with Mark.

52
00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:03.910
Mark, hello.

53
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.510
Welcome to Office, ladies.

54
00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:08.050
Thank you guys for having me.

55
00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:09.550
I really appreciate it.

56
00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.290
Well, we are so excited to talk to you.

57
00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:19.250
We are really enjoying Nate, and Nate had some real fun stuff coming up, and we just know the fans can't wait to hear from you.

58
00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.130
Well, that's exciting.

59
00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:26.570
I've been waiting a long time to get my chance to talk on Office, ladies, so here we go.

60
00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:37.990
Well, Mark, when your character was introduced, we told a little bit of your origin story, but I just want you to tell it again in your own words from the beginning.

61
00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:40.470
This is our favorite question to ask our guests.

62
00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.550
How did you get your job on the office?

63
00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:46.890
I quite honestly backed into it.

64
00:03:46.890 --> 00:04:03.470
I was unemployed, living in Wisconsin, in Milwaukee, and I had this idea to make my friends laugh, which was go on morning news shows as a character,

65
00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:09.690
and a character that would be inept and clumsy.

66
00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:17.190
But the main thing was that the anchors and the news station had no idea I was in character.

67
00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:21.410
Now, I had never taken acting classes or anything like that.

68
00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:32.150
I just thought it would be funny, and so I would go on these morning shows as this yo-yo master, which is funny.

69
00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:38.070
Kay Strass, the yo-yo master, who was not a master of the yo-yo.

70
00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.910
No, because I can't yo-yo.

71
00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:43.050
I'm not even a big fan of yo-yoing.

72
00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:56.250
And so I would get on and start rambling about my chaotic personal life, and finally they would ask me to do a demonstration, and that would go horribly wrong.

73
00:04:56.250 --> 00:05:09.070
The idea was to do 10 or 11 or 12 of these, and if you watched them all in a row, you would get this kind of strange story arc about this guy's life, all told through two-minute,

74
00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:11.610
one-minute segments on morning shows.

75
00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:15.810
Now, I have to stop you because you're saying you never took any acting classes.

76
00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:20.870
Did you take, like, improv or sketch comedy?

77
00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:21.390
No?

78
00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:23.090
No, I never did any of that.

79
00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.610
It's like a writing class, because there is a real story arc there.

80
00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:34.530
Yeah, it's, no, you know, all that was just myself wanting to do this.

81
00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.830
I'm so fascinated by this.

82
00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:39.790
I always have been.

83
00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:45.590
I've made you tell me this story so many times, because every time, it just blows my mind.

84
00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:47.010
Okay, so I'll let you go on.

85
00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:52.810
Okay, so I did these appearances, and like I said,

86
00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:56.570
the idea was to do 10, 11, 12, and to get a story arc,

87
00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:02.530
and what I didn't expect was for people to start posting them on YouTube.

88
00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:11.690
And this was back in 2010, and so there were still videos that could really catch fire and become viral.

89
00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:15.770
And the K-Strauss morning show appearances became viral.

90
00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:26.130
And at first, I was really annoyed, because I was like, well, they've ruined my opportunity here to tell, like, this story arc I want to tell.

91
00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:32.050
Because I had no idea of, you know, coming to L.A., coming to Hollywood.

92
00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:41.330
And oddly enough, a friend of mine got a text message from one of the writers on the show,

93
00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:50.170
and she figured out who I was through computer sleuthing and what have you.

94
00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:58.710
And she asked if I, you know, would I want to talk to Paul Lieberstein, who was running the show.

95
00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:01.690
And I was like, yeah, of course I would.

96
00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:05.070
And a few minutes later, Paul calls me.

97
00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:06.290
Oh, my gosh.

98
00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.090
And all of a sudden, I'm talking to Toby from the office.

99
00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:15.250
And a month later, they flew me out.

100
00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:16.470
I met with the writers.

101
00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.850
And a month after that, I was on the show.

102
00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:21.930
I mean, that's crazy.

103
00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.710
The real crazy thing here.

104
00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:24.490
Yeah.

105
00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:35.930
The writer that reached out was my now wife, Amelie Gillette, who had just started writing on the show a couple weeks before all this blew up.

106
00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:43.170
So you got an arc on the office and a wife out of this.

107
00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:43.910
The yo-yo.

108
00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:47.870
The yo-yo idea that was just meant to entertain your friends.

109
00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:49.850
It's an incredible story.

110
00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:54.210
You know, you don't hear too many stories like this anymore.

111
00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:58.310
You know, it's usually, oh, I was working for 12, 15 years.

112
00:07:58.730 --> 00:07:58.970
Yeah.

113
00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.570
You know, I started in commercials and what have you.

114
00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:06.010
And I feel kind of guilty because it was kind of overnight almoSt.

115
00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:10.250
But I'm so grateful that it all happened.

116
00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:15.190
I got so much out of just making a jackass of myself on morning news.

117
00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:21.790
So you had seen The Office before you got cast, obviously.

118
00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:22.370
Oh, yeah.

119
00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.990
What was it like to walk on to the set?

120
00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:30.030
You know, the first person I met from the cast was Rain.

121
00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:31.450
And.

122
00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:32.770
I'm sorry.

123
00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:33.730
I'm so sorry.

124
00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:36.870
I can't imagine what that was like.

125
00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:42.230
Now, you guys can say that because he is the sweetest, nicest, most genuine person.

126
00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:43.010
I know.

127
00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:44.650
He's just a good, good soul.

128
00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:45.270
Exactly.

129
00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:49.650
And so, you know, it could have gone to one of two ways.

130
00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:54.650
And after now I've been acting for, you know, quite a few years now.

131
00:08:54.650 --> 00:09:07.250
You know, I and I've as a guest star have gone on many shows, the cast is either welcoming or they can be a little cold and a little distance because here comes a new face.

132
00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:11.770
And that's time away from you as an actor and your character.

133
00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:20.710
Or you can just, you know, a high tide lifts all boats type scenario where they're happy that there's fresh people coming in.

134
00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:24.190
And you guys were so nice and welcoming to me.

135
00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:31.290
I really got spoiled coming into the show because of that, because you guys were so nice and welcoming.

136
00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:35.290
And what a first show to cut your teeth on as an actor.

137
00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:41.730
I mean, everyone was so generous and encouraging from the top down.

138
00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:46.810
It was I look back on it and I was so lucky.

139
00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:48.350
So very, very fortunate.

140
00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:55.850
Well, you know, this episode we're breaking down is the lotto, but we all felt like we won the lottery ticket, you know, the minute we were caSt.

141
00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:56.310
Yeah.

142
00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:00.010
So I love hearing that because that's how we felt on set.

143
00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:05.230
We were just really happy to be there and counted ourselves lucky every day.

144
00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:13.450
But, Mark, we wanted to ask you if you have any favorite memories or behind the scene moment or anything you would want to share from your time on the show.

145
00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:16.130
Gosh, there's so many.

146
00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:28.050
You know, any time I could get one of you guys to break, I was a feather in my cap, especially as, you know, a person just starting out in acting.

147
00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:36.130
I felt like, you know, there was a scene I remember with Jenna where she's grilling me.

148
00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:40.370
And I think it was in season nine, vandalism.

149
00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:41.870
We're down in the warehouse.

150
00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:42.610
Yeah.

151
00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:45.390
It's one of my strongest memories of breaking.

152
00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.950
And we just had so much fun.

153
00:10:48.990 --> 00:11:03.810
And it's such a fond memory for me that that moment, you know, the first day on the show or my first time ever in front of a set in front of a crew filming.

154
00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:10.030
Um, I had to knock down a beehive that was in front of the office.

155
00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:10.570
Yeah.

156
00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:17.170
And that was the first time I had ever acted in my life, uh, professionally or in front of a crew.

157
00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:24.550
And all of a sudden you're, you're in front of 35 people, 40 people all looking at you.

158
00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:31.750
And I just remember thinking to myself, well, either you can do this or you can't, but there's no real going back.

159
00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:34.030
So you might as well give it your all.

160
00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:44.170
And I had, it's one of those moments where you're both terrified, but having so much fun and it just felt right.

161
00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:48.130
And I had never had a moment like that in my, in my life.

162
00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:48.930
Wow.

163
00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:55.210
Well, I just remember all of us being very enamored with you in scenes.

164
00:11:55.210 --> 00:12:03.550
You were this character and you had this way about you that was so unique and so deeply funny.

165
00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:07.170
It was so deeply funny to me, Mark, the way you did Nate.

166
00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:09.250
It was so earneSt.

167
00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:09.850
Yes.

168
00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:10.930
So earneSt.

169
00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:11.910
And you had.

170
00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:12.450
And real.

171
00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:16.710
And yes, you, you would do this thing that Steve did.

172
00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:18.510
That was really hard for me.

173
00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.750
You have a look that comes across your face.

174
00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:25.090
I can't really explain it, but Steve has it as well.

175
00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:31.990
And if I catch your eye, when you kind of do this, it's this expression of like, huh, I'm a goner.

176
00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:33.170
I am a goner.

177
00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:38.070
And so there were a few actors on set that I couldn't make eye contact with really directly.

178
00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:41.170
And you and Steve are like top two.

179
00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:42.650
Well, good Lord.

180
00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:43.430
That's high praise.

181
00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:44.590
Yeah.

182
00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:49.610
I, you know, it, it may just be my dumb face, but I'll take the compliment.

183
00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:50.170
Thank you.

184
00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:54.830
It just, I believed everything you were saying.

185
00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:55.870
That's what it is.

186
00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:05.090
In rewatching the show, another scene that I remember that was just insane that I got to be in with you as well was the,

187
00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:12.770
the toilet paper machine scenes where you had to unply and reply the toilet paper.

188
00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:13.870
Do you remember that thing?

189
00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:15.030
Oh, absolutely.

190
00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:20.510
That was, that's one of the most reference scenes I get when people come up to me.

191
00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:28.050
Um, but yeah, that I, I just remember being shown the contraption and how to work it.

192
00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:33.910
And I just thought, wow, this is, this is a lot of work for a quick joke.

193
00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:44.150
And that's why I love the office is that a lot of times there would, there would be a lot of, of groundwork ahead of the joke, especially for these physical jokes,

194
00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:49.410
like the replying and, and unplying of the toilet paper machine.

195
00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:54.190
Yeah, that honestly, it's, it's the most reference scene that I get.

196
00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:55.350
I believe it.

197
00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:58.930
We have people like wrote in that asked us how it was built.

198
00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:02.590
Like they're just fascinated by all of it and Phil Shea in the props department.

199
00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:11.570
Yeah, you know, it, it's one of those things that I look back on and I, again, I think, gosh, what a show to just come in on that.

200
00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:13.650
Everyone's loving what they're doing.

201
00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:22.610
Every department was in love with the show and you don't realize how rare that is until you start working on other stuff.

202
00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:27.150
And they didn't look at it as just a nine to five.

203
00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:31.530
They really loved everyone involved and loved what they were doing.

204
00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:34.830
And it's, it shows on the screen, I think today.

205
00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:41.130
When people ask you about your time on the office, what are some of the most frequently asked questions?

206
00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:42.870
Like, what do people want to know?

207
00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:46.690
People would often ask, did you guys break all the time?

208
00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:50.670
Was it always really fun to be on set?

209
00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:59.350
But, and I, I, I also get a lot of, you know, are, are the characters like that in real life?

210
00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:01.430
Are, are the actors like their characters?

211
00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:10.970
Which is still fascinating today that we get these types of questions because, you know, you hear stories of people writing into, I love Lucy,

212
00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:14.870
being mad at Ricky and first treating his wife that way.

213
00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:19.230
And you're like, gosh, that was such a quaint, naive time, but it still happens.

214
00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:22.110
People are like, you know, is Angela really uptight?

215
00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:24.810
And it's like, no, not really at all.

216
00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:29.670
You know, and, you know, is Mindy really like that?

217
00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:31.590
No, Mindy's not really like that.

218
00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:32.810
Maybe a little.

219
00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:33.710
Maybe a little.

220
00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:35.750
Is, is Brian dumb?

221
00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:38.290
It's like, no, Brian's a very smart guy.

222
00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:38.970
He's incredibly smart.

223
00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:39.290
Yeah.

224
00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:55.670
Because I'm, you know, 10 years heavier and doughier than I was back then.

225
00:15:56.570 --> 00:16:00.010
The really surprising thing is the age.

226
00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:04.610
And I'm sure you guys are getting this as well, probably far more than I am.

227
00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:12.170
Younger people that come up to me, teenagers and people in their early 20s who love the show.

228
00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:22.210
So we were in Italy just last week and I went into a wine shop there and it was a young guy

